# Vendored crates, and the published release that ends each entry's reason. One per line:
#
#   <vendor/ child> <crate> <version vendored> <ISO date checked> <what a newer release means here>
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A vendored copy is a decision that expires, and nothing here could see it
# expire. `VENDOR.md:11`'s actionlint row states the hole in its own words - *"Temporary until the
# locked actionlint release contains this commit; nothing detects that release automatically"* - and
# `devco/arrow-majors-allow`'s duckdb row is the same shape: the fix merged upstream, the newest
# published release predates it, and the entry waits on a release nobody is watching. A vendored
# crate is invisible to `cargo update` and to a dependency bot by construction, because it is a path
# dependency, so the one signal that would normally arrive never does.
#
# THE TWO MECHANISMS OVER IT, and neither does the other's job.
#
#   * `cargo xtask check-vendor-expiry` (`just vendor-expiry`) reads this file, asks
#     `index.crates.io` for each crate's newest non-yanked release, and FAILS when that release is
#     above the version vendored. It needs the network, so it is deliberately NOT in the `hygiene`
#     sweep and not in `just validate` - those are hermetic, and a gate that reddens an unrelated
#     branch at whatever moment upstream publishes is worse than the rot it fixes. `just update` runs
#     it, which is the moment somebody is already deciding about versions.
#   * `cargo xtask check-workflows`, hermetically, holds this file COMPLETE: every immediate child of
#     `vendor/` is named by a row here, and every row names a child that exists. That is what stops
#     the next vendored tree arriving with no expiry declaration at all - see
#     `vendored_tree_problems` in `xtask/src/workflows/scorecard.rs`, beside the rules that already
#     require a `REUSE.toml` block and a `VENDOR.md` record for the same child.
#
# WHAT NEITHER COVERS, stated next to the claim. A newer release is not proof the reason expired: it
# says re-read the row, not retire it. The reverse is worse and is the real limit - NO newer release
# is not proof the reason still holds, because an upstream project can abandon a fix or ship it
# under a version scheme this file cannot compare. The DATE is not parsed by anything; the position
# must be filled and a current-looking date from a year ago is on you. And this covers vendored
# CRATES only: `nix/actionlint.nix` is a patch against a Go release rather than a registry crate,
# and `devco/arrow-majors-allow` is an allowlist rather than a copy, so neither has a row here. A
# second source kind - a GitHub release tag - is where actionlint would go, and it is not built.
#
# A version with a non-numeric component (a prerelease, a build metadata suffix) is refused rather
# than guessed at, in this file and in what the index returns.

